in 1978 the world was still in the grip  of star wars fever so how did everyone  react when the man of steel himself hit  theaters keep watching to find out  even though superman had never featured  in a big budget movie before he was  still an icon one who had appeared in  nearly every other medium imaginable  over the previous 40 years viewers had  built up a very definite image of who  the man of steel was and that meant the  movie had a lot to live up to the  producers turned that handicap into an  asset the same way mgm had with gone  with the wind decades earlier by  promoting superman with a hyped up  global talent search warner brothers was  so determined to get the movie right  that they spent two years on the casting  search which was almost as long as the  rest of the production put together the  whole process was so public that roger  ebert made sure to mention it in his  contemporary review and though hollywood  had done this often enough to make him  skeptical he wrote although talent  searches are usually 100 horse feathers  this time for once they actually found  the right guy  christopher reeve had never appeared in  a movie before but according to producer  elia salkin he won out over hundreds of  other unknowns as well as superstars  like paul newman steve mcqueen and  arnold schwarzenegger  superman hit the screen at just the  right moment in film history it began  pre-production at a bearish time for  sci-fi blockbusters hollywood was still  recovering from its near collapse in the  60s and the few hits it managed to pull  out were mostly dark dramas like the  exorcist and the godfather as for  science fiction audiences mostly had to  choose between two flavors b movie  makers were filling drive-ins with cheap  silly adventures full of laughable  effects and silly  [Music]  performances meanwhile their upmarket  competitors were making more polished  products but these tended to be grim  philosophical movies like soylent green  and 2001 a space odyssey there wasn't  much in between the year before superman  made it to theaters however another  movie changed the whole game star wars  shattered records by combining an  old-fashioned space opera with  newfangled effects technology it proved  moviegoers were ready to have fun again  to the tune of hundreds of millions of  dollars and that's not even counting the  film's absolutely massive merchandising  takings audiences wanted more and while  other studios scrambled to cash in  superman offered the perfect movie to  tide them over while they waited for  star wars 2. warner brothers even  poached the star wars crew for talent to  bring their own vision to life including  the now iconic composer john williams  warner brothers knew what they had with  superman and they didn't want to miss  out on any potential viewers so the  studio made sure everybody and their  mother knew the man of steel was coming  to a theater near them mcleans reported  on the movie's marketing just before it  came out in their appropriately named  super hype cover story according to the  magazine warner brothers estimates that  north americans will have had 7 billion  chances through print radio and tv  advertising to have heard about superman  mclean's placed the movie's advertising  budget somewhere between 6 million to 7  million over 26 million in today's money  the story even runs down a few of the  tie-ins that were available at the time  from books and t-shirts to toys and some  stranger items such as trash cans it's  hard to imagine a scenario in which all  that investment wouldn't pay off but it  was a very real possibility in the  mclean's piece writer ivor davis  reflects on past movies where things  went wrong including a remake of king  kong and the great gatsby adaptation  starring robert redford these movies had  equally massive ad campaigns and equally  safe bet source material but failed to  reach the sky high profit margins the  hype men dreamed of  superheroes had moved off the printed  page and into the movies before 1978.  after so many decades of silver screen  crime fighters however it can be hard to  imagine the impact superman had when it  hit theaters live-action superheroes  have been around almost as long as their  comics counterparts starting with 40s  cereals based on captain marvel batman  and superman himself but hollywood  didn't have enough faith in the source  material to give these projects the kind  of resources they would in 1978 let  alone now instead viewers got baggy  costumes cardboard sets and laughable  plots that barely resembled those found  in the comics until superman the biggest  splash superheroes had made in the media  was in 1966 with the batman tv show  starring adam west holy holy donut  but that couldn't be more different from  what director richard donner did with  superman batman wasn't an adaptation so  much as an officially approved parody  goofing on the whole idea of superheroes  by exaggerating their wackiest aspects  and by the 70s tv super heroing had  moved on to shows like wonder woman and  the incredible hulk which were less  explicitly comedic but still full of  sophie's silliness audiences probably  expected more of the same from superman  but that's not what they got at all the  film treats superman as serious business  and gives his adventures far more  grandeur than other live-action heroes  had enjoyed up to that point anyone who  ever got seriously invested in the  straight face superhero flicks of later  decades owes donner a very large debt  indeed  close encounters of the third kind and  star wars made it possible to bring  moviegoers a whole new kind of superman  earlier versions had led a lot to be  desired in the special effects  department of course the movie's serials  of the 40s had suddenly transitioned to  animation for the flying scenes and in  the 50s tv show actor george reeves hung  from a wire while a fan blew in his face  it took until 1977 for the movies to  finally catch up with fans imaginations  and the filmmakers pulled out all the  stops to make it work the flying scenes  on superman required a combination of  matte effects camera trickery  complicated rigs and physical stunt work  in the form of having to hang  christopher reeve up in the air  sometimes as high as 50 feet the  marketing team summed up the film crew's  accomplishment in a simple phrase that  everyone in america knew by heart by the  time the movie premiered you'll believe  a man can fly there were plenty of other  impressive effects too superman lifted  the innovations of star wars to create a  miniature planet krypton and a  crystalline fortress of solitude that  looked just as real as tatooine or the  death star but that tagline sums up  everything that makes superman work as a  movie suddenly the sight of a live actor  as a comic book hero tights and all was  a cause for awe instead of laughter  when superman was released audiences  were ready for some good old-fashioned  fun star wars had revived the long dead  space opera genre of course but it also  self-consciously nodded at the movies of  the past with techniques such as the  wipe transition and an opening crawl  borrowed from the serials of george  lucas's childhood lucas would later lean  on those retro influences again in his  story for steven spielberg's raiders of  the lost ark superman was part of that  same zeitgeist the special effects were  downright futuristic in 1978 and donner  used them to make a deeply old-fashioned  story fresh again that's certainly how  roger ebert felt at the time writing  superman is a pure delight a wondrous  combination of all the old-fashioned  things we never really get tired of  adventure and romance heroes and  villains earth-shaking special effects  and you know what else wit donner  self-consciously leaned into that aspect  of superman calling on classic stars  like veteran western hero glenn ford as  paw kent and former little rascal jackie  cooper as perry white and the scenes of  superman's youth in smallville kansas  are self-consciously timeless with  classic cars and settings that could  have come out of a norman rockwell  painting as clark kent superman still  wears the same blue suit and fedora he  had in the 40s and while his co-workers  may be more fashionable the atmosphere  at the daily planet nevertheless evokes  the classic newspaper movies of yore  with the benefit of hindsight it's hard  to imagine that superman was anything  but a super safe bet but that wasn't how  audiences felt in 1978 the movie  represented an enormous investment with  its high-tech effects and all-star cast  not to mention the millions upon  millions of dollars warner brothers  poured into the ad campaign all this for  a movie in a genre that didn't really  exist yet too and had only really proven  its viability in other media no doubt  the fate of superman was up in the air  until the final box office tallies came  rolling in in the mclean's article about  the movie it was discussed how warner  brothers might share the prophets of the  movie the article mentions that fact is  only relevant if the movie takes off and  calls that the biggest if in movie  history it goes on to say the  consequences of a super flop could be  dire since warner communications had  been caught in the stock market crash of  1973. in the piece mclean's quotes an  executive who reassures them that the  company is turning a steady profit in  many sectors but they still conclude the  money from a successful movie and from  all the merchandising could make all the  difference apparently it did warner  communications now a t warner media is  bigger than ever and superman is still a  major part of their plans i could have  had  class i could have been a contender  i could have been somebody  superman had plenty of star power even  beyond the name recognition of its  fictional hero and no star in the cast  was bigger than marlon brando previously  brando had defined 50s youth culture and  the new school of method acting and  early hits such as on the waterfront and  a streetcar named desire and he was  bigger than ever in 1978 after his  oscar-winning performance in the  blockbuster gangster epic the godfather  that meant the ads for superman made  sure to put his involvement in the movie  front and center viewers could even be  forgiven for thinking he was the star so  they must have gotten a rude awakening  when after a brief brando-centric  prologue he disappears for most of the  movie only making a few more appearances  as a recording to mentor his son  altogether he only gets about 20 minutes  of screen time and he knocked out the  whole job in less than two weeks of  course at the height of his fame brandon  was able to command a hefty payday for a  few days work and with superman he  scored over 3 million and  11.75 percent of all the movies future  profits no wonder warner brothers wanted  to make the most of their investment by  hyping up the star's involvement  the producers behind superman had a lot  of faith in their movie and so they made  an unprecedented gamble on its success  before they even knew for sure that one  superman movie could sell they committed  themselves to two filming both the film  and its sequel simultaneously that may  not seem unusual now that every major  movie gets at least a sequel or two but  in 1978 it was unheard of star wars was  still just a single movie in 1978 and  only a few films had ever been  successfully franchised and most of  those few like the james bond and  godzilla franchises were pretty clearly  making things up as they went the  closest precedent was the godfather part  2 and there's a huge difference between  a character-driven historical epic and  an action franchise but superman did  learn from the godfather no doubt  because the 2-series shared a  screenwriter in mario puzo like puzzo's  other movies and unlike previous  franchises superman treated both movies  as one big story a long sequence at the  beginning deals with terence stamp as  the kryptonian criminal general zod who  wouldn't become important until superman  2. if the sequel had never been made  it's easy to imagine audiences  scratching their heads over what he was  even doing there granted some behind the  scenes drama eventually forced director  richard donner to focus on just the  first film and he was ousted when it  came time to finish part two but even if  superman didn't quite invent the sequel  teaser it came close and audiences  apparently ate it up superman 2 was the  number one movie in the world when it  hit theaters in 1981. check out one of  our newest videos right here plus even  more looper videos about your favorite  movies are coming soon subscribe to our  youtube channel and hit the bell so you  don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

